Core idea: A web app where people with zero animation experience can create playful, shareable animations — think "Canva meets Lottie" rather than a professional motion-design tool.

Target user: Casual creators — people making GIFs/stickers for social media, greeting cards, presentation flourishes, simple explainer clips. Not professional animators.

1. Core features (MVP)
Template-first start: users pick from pre-built animation templates (bouncing text, particle bursts, character walk cycles, celebratory confetti, etc.) rather than a blank canvas
Drag-and-drop timeline: simple keyframe editor — position, scale, rotation, opacity — with easing presets (bounce, elastic, ease-in-out) instead of raw curve editing
Asset library: built-in shapes, emoji/sticker packs, simple character rigs, background patterns
Live preview: instant playback as you edit
Export: GIF, MP4, and Lottie/JSON (for embedding in apps/websites)
2. Nice-to-have (v2)
Text-to-animation ("type a phrase, get a title-card animation")
Simple physics presets (gravity, bounce, spring)
Collaborative/shareable projects with a public gallery
Sound effects synced to keyframes
3. Suggested tech stack (web-based, given you're a web dev)
Frontend: React + Motion (formerly Framer Motion) for UI-driven animation, or GSAP for the timeline engine — GSAP is more powerful for building an actual editor
Canvas rendering: Konva.js or PixiJS for the drawable/animatable canvas layer
Timeline UI: build custom on top of a library like react-timeline-editor or roll your own with GSAP's timeline API
Export: gif.js or ffmpeg.wasm for GIF/MP4 rendering in-browser; Lottie export via bodymovin-style JSON if you support vector animation
Backend: lightweight (Node/Express or serverless) mainly for saving projects, assets, and rendering export jobs that are too heavy for the browser
4. Build order
Static canvas + object placement (no animation yet)
Basic keyframe system (position/scale/opacity over time)
Timeline UI + playback
Easing presets + templates
Export (GIF first — simplest), then MP4/Lottie
Asset library and polish
